WEBVTT

00:00:00.469 --> 00:00:05.165
Welcome to Alaventra. Rank in
search. Get recommended by AI.

00:00:06.143 --> 00:00:11.718
In tutorial 6.5, you'll connect your
CMS, the system that runs your website,

00:00:11.768 --> 00:00:14.423
so written pages arrive there as drafts.

00:00:15.463 --> 00:00:19.264
First, open Website settings,
and choose the Publishing tab.

00:00:21.442 --> 00:00:24.267
Written pages go to your CMS as drafts,

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:29.232
and only an owner or admin can
publish one live. Click Connect a CMS.

00:00:30.286 --> 00:00:36.021
Next, pick your CMS: WordPress,
Webflow, Contentful, or a webhook,

00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:39.323
a link your developer sets up,
for any other website system.

00:00:40.328 --> 00:00:42.623
For WordPress, check your site address,

00:00:42.673 --> 00:00:45.603
then type your user name
and an application password.

00:00:47.853 --> 00:00:52.208
Not sure where to find it? Show me how
walks you through creating one in WordPress.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:57.371
Optionally, add the address
pattern of a live page,

00:00:57.421 --> 00:01:00.276
so every published page
can be checked and tracked.

00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:05.882
Then click Connect and test. Alaventra
checks the connection straight away,

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:08.631
and your CMS appears
on the Publishing tab.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:13.135
It shows as Connected, with
the time of its last test.

00:01:13.185 --> 00:01:15.892
Click Test connection any
time to check it again.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.085
With Webflow or Contentful,

00:01:19.135 --> 00:01:23.499
the first test also reads the
fields your CMS uses for a page.

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:28.733
Choose which field holds each part
of the page, then click Save mapping.

00:01:28.783 --> 00:01:30.228
You only do this once.

00:01:32.508 --> 00:01:38.318
So, open Publishing and choose Connect a
CMS, pick your CMS and paste its details,

00:01:38.368 --> 00:01:41.360
connect and test, and
map the fields if asked.

00:01:42.354 --> 00:01:49.486
Your CMS is connected. Next, send a page
to your CMS as a draft, in tutorial 6.6.
